################################################################################
# Notes and Best Practices
################################################################################
#
# 1. VERSION CONTROL
#    - This example file SHOULD be committed to version control
#    - Your terraform.tfvars file should NOT be committed (contains real values)
#    - The .gitignore file excludes terraform.tfvars by default
#
# 2. SECRETS MANAGEMENT
#    - Never put sensitive data in this file or terraform.tfvars
#    - Use AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store for secrets
#    - Reference secrets via data sources in Terraform code
#
# 3. ENVIRONMENT-SPECIFIC CONFIGURATIONS
#    - Create separate .tfvars files for each environment:
#      * dev.tfvars
#      * staging.tfvars
#      * production.tfvars
#    - Use: terraform plan -var-file=dev.tfvars
#
# 4. VALIDATION
#    - Run terraform validate to check for errors
#    - Run terraform plan to preview changes
#    - Review the plan carefully before applying
#
# 5. COST MANAGEMENT
#    - Estimate costs before deployment using AWS Pricing Calculator
#    - Monitor costs in AWS Cost Explorer after deployment
#    - Tag resources consistently for accurate cost allocation
#    - Use smaller instance types for development environments
#
# 6. SECURITY
#    - Follow principle of least privilege
#    - Enable MFA for AWS accounts
#    - Use separate AWS accounts for different environments
#    - Regularly review IAM permissions and security groups
#
# 7. DOCUMENTATION
#    - Document your specific configuration choices
#    - Keep comments up to date when changing values
#    - Share knowledge with your team about customizations
#
################################################################################
